 "interpretation": {
  "headline": "Asking a question instead of comparing similarity gives a cleaner and lower answer: exact 90.5%, day summary 61.9%, week summary 33.3% on the same 21 probes.",
  "the_discriminating_number": {
   "exact": {
    "unknown": 0,
    "answered_wrongly": 2
   },
   "day-summary": {
    "unknown": 8,
    "answered_wrongly": 0
   },
   "week-summary": {
    "unknown": 14,
    "answered_wrongly": 0
   }
  },
  "why_that_split_matters": "The two failure modes are different in kind. At the summary levels every failure is UNKNOWN — the model was told to say UNKNOWN when the context lacks the answer, and it did — so those are information losses in the summary itself. At the exact level there are zero UNKNOWNs and two wrong answers: the answer is present in the retrieved text, consistently ('option A' appears twice for the probe that was answered B), so that is a reader limitation, not a memory one. Conflating the two would misattribute a model error to the store.",
  "against_the_token_metric": "Token survival reports 85% quantity retention at the day level; QA reports 61.9% answerability on the same period. The lexical metric therefore OVERSTATES what survives, not understates it — the opposite of the direction the reviewer anticipated when warning that lexical counting might miss paraphrase-preserved facts. A bound worth stating: these probes ask for a single letter, which token survival happens to describe well, so the gap is a lower bound on the divergence rather than a general law.",
  "limits": "21 probes, one seed, one reader (qwen3.5-4b at temperature 0). The reader is the same model family that wrote the summaries, which could flatter them; an independent reader would be the fair test. The exact-level reader errors show the reader is not merely echoing the summary.",
  "separability_check": "The embedding separability guard added in semantic_survival.py applies to that script; these probes do not use embeddings at all, so a misconfigured endpoint cannot affect them."
 }
